CHAPTER 2
LITERATURE SURVEY

2.1. Overview of the Research Area

There has been an increasing interest in the field of optimizing EV charging due to the rapid uptake of EVs. With the growing need for charging services, there has been an increasing interest in studying efficient management of EV charging within the fields of energy systems, battery management, smart grid, and machine learning [3, 4]. Notably, the aim is to ensure efficient energy utilization, effective cost management, and maintenance of battery health in addition to ensuring convenient charging.

Various parameters can affect EV charging performance [10]. Some of these parameters include the time spent during charging, starting state of charge, battery capacity, type of charging station (slow, fast, or rapid chargers), pricing scheme for electricity, ambient temperature, and user behavior during charging. Notably, each of these factors interplays with others in complex manners. Therefore, determining the optimal way to perform charging activities has remained challenging. For instance, charging activities may be expensive when done during peak hours or adversely affect the battery health when done frequently through fast chargers.

In order to overcome these challenges, scientists have recently been utilizing machine learning methods, which allow analyzing big amounts of data and finding complex hidden patterns that cannot be detected using conventional approaches. Machine learning models predict such factors as battery wear and tear, energy consumption, and charging demands, thus facilitating more accurate decision-making processes [2, 5, 7].

Concepts included in the field of study are data analysis, prediction, optimization, and life cycle management of batteries. The use of data analysis technologies will help to identify and solve existing issues with previous charging patterns. The process of prediction uses available information about possible future occurrences. When conducting an optimization procedure, scientists can compare different solutions to choose the best possible option. Finally, the management of battery life cycles presupposes maintenance of its perfect conditions.
In addition to that, it should be noted that this topic is also linked to the idea of smart grids, which presuppose the application of a charging scheduling strategy depending on the total load of the grid system, as such a measure can help prevent grid overload [11]. In other words, through optimization, it becomes possible to ensure a stable operation of the system by adjusting the consumption of electricity during off-peak hours and applying renewable energy resources.

Overall, the field under analysis has evolved from pure observations of the process of charging electric vehicles to the development of intelligent systems that help make decisions in regard to this issue. Specifically, these systems are responsible not only for the analysis of the process itself, but also provide recommendations to EV drivers regarding the optimal timing and the choice of chargers.

2.2. Existing Models and Frameworks

Different types of models have been employed for the purpose of EV charging optimization in previous studies.

Rule-based systems and mathematical optimization algorithms constitute the conventional type of approach that has been applied to schedule EV charging based on certain restrictions like charging duration, electricity price, and grid load. While being applicable in specific environments, this kind of methodology might prove ineffective when dealing with the changes in actual charging practices [3, 4].

Battery Management Systems (BMS) play an essential role in modern EVs. The main idea behind BMS is to collect information regarding the key indicators like voltage, current, temperature, and state of charge. However, there is little use of BMS in terms of optimizing the behavior of users and charging practice [1].

Finally, machine learning techniques can be regarded as the most efficient due to their capability of modeling complicated and nonlinear dependencies. Different kinds of regression models such as Linear Regression, Random Forest Regressor, Gradient Boosting, and XGBoost have been utilized to predict charging cost, energy consumption, duration, and battery degradation. Out of all those models, tree-based methods appear particularly advantageous for structured charging dataset [2, 6].

Newer architectures include intelligent charging approaches, IoT-based monitoring systems and cloud analytics for electric vehicles. Their aim is to give real-time insight into charging situations and make recommendations accordingly. Unfortunately, they often lack user-centric design and focus on fleet management or infrastructural optimization [4, 7].

2.3. Limitations Identified from Literature Survey (Research Gaps)

As revealed through the literature survey, there are some major research areas where current approaches lack in terms of efficiency, which has justified the development of the proposed approach.

First, many researchers have been concentrating on developing models aimed at predicting charging loads, estimating the degree of battery degradation, as well as examining energy usage patterns. Though all those models have proved to be quite useful from a theoretical point of view, most of them fail to provide any further guidance regarding how end users should act upon their findings in order to optimize the charging process. Hence, the users remain confused about the ways in which they can benefit from using such models [2, 5].

In addition to this, a great number of methods use datasets that are proprietary in their nature, including proprietary parameters of the battery or onboard diagnostic information, or some infrastructure information, which is not always accessible to outsiders. As a result, it becomes difficult to validate the conclusions or reproduce the results, thus limiting the application possibilities of the proposed models. In order to increase the applicability of predictive charging techniques, it is crucial to create algorithms capable of functioning in any circumstances and utilizing publicly available information.

Third, many researchers focus on optimizing only one aspect, either minimizing the cost of charging or maximizing the state of the battery at the end of a particular period of time. Nevertheless, in the context of real-life applications of electric cars, both aspects mentioned above are strongly interconnected and sometimes even opposed to each other. For example, the low cost of charging implies the possibility of charging in certain conditions that may have a negative impact on the battery's state.

Fourth, another methodological limitation noted by some papers includes the poor management of data leakage during the model training phase and the evaluation phase. The data collected for EV charging is temporal in nature, and inappropriate division of this data into train and test sets will inevitably lead to leakage of information about the future in the training phase. This will create overly optimistic predictions that do not translate well into reality, and thus appropriate techniques must be used to split the dataset and perform the testing phase to ensure accurate model performance [8].

Fifth, there is a general deficiency among many studies of having a clearly defined application or deployment phase for the algorithm or model. Even if the model performs exceptionally well on the data and produces great predictive power, it is only ever mentioned in terms of experimental results or performance metrics. However, without a good user interface, the user will never be able to interact with the model to use its output effectively.

Moreover, many currently available models lack transparency and interpretation of outcomes. It might be hard for users to comprehend the results if the logic used for recommendations is not clear enough. The use of visual aids can help to improve transparency and thus increase trust in outcomes and the whole process.

The final gap identified is related to the lack of attention towards the integration of all elements into one system. While many papers discuss some of them separately, such as prediction, optimization, or visualization, an integrated model is needed that will include these elements in order to deliver an effective tool.

It can be said that the current gaps formed the main reasons for the development of this project. The project itself is aimed at filling in the gaps as discussed above in terms of predictive modeling, optimization, proper methodology evaluation, and implementation in the form of an application. Thus, the project serves as both a predictive model and a decision support system helping to charge electric vehicles in a smart way.
