2.5. Plan of Action

There was a defined process for executing the project to ensure successful development, creation of a model, and evaluation of the system. The processes involved ensured continuity from the beginning through to the end of the project.

The first phase entailed recognizing the challenge at hand and gaining an insight into the domain of the project. At this stage, all the challenges concerning electric vehicle charging, such as poor charging patterns, lack of knowledge among users, and inadequate decision support systems, were considered. A clear insight into the parameters affecting battery life and energy efficiency during charging was gained.

The next phase consisted of data collection and analysis. In this context, the data set for EV charging was collected and analyzed to ascertain the nature and distribution of certain characteristics. Some of the key characteristics included state of charge (SOC), charging duration, temperature, and energy consumption.
Subsequently, data pre-processing and cleaning activities were performed on the data set before developing any models. The data pre-processing and cleaning consisted of:
Timestamp extraction and formatting
Management of missing or inconsistent data
Data value and type validation
Outlier detection and correction
This made sure that the data set was ready to be used in machine learning programs.

In order to ensure the integrity of the model development process, chronological data split was used. Due to the fact that EV charging data depends on time, chronological splits of the training and test datasets were made as opposed to random split. The approach chosen for the data split did not allow any data leakage to occur.
	
The next step was related to feature engineering, which implied the creation of new features that were expected to improve the performance of the models. These features comprised SOC-based features denoting battery status, charging rates, time features (peak/off-peak times and time of the day), and user-related behavioral features.

Afterwards, machine learning algorithms were created using the regression technique. The two algorithms created are the Random Forest Regression algorithm, known for its stability and capability to identify the relationship between variables even when they are non-linear, and the XGBoost Regression algorithm, known for its high efficiency and accuracy. The aim for creating these algorithms is to predict specific outcomes on charging efficiency, battery aging, and system efficiency. The above machine learning algorithms were compared against each other. This is because their efficiencies and accuracies were analyzed. After analyzing the results, the most efficient model was selected.

Upon developing the predictive models, optimization logic was also designed. In particular, the logic was used to create various scenarios related to battery charging by tweaking certain elements like charging duration and charging type. Different scenarios were tested through the use of the trained machine learning model, and the best scenario identified based on pre-defined performance metrics.

Optimization logic and the machine learning models created were then incorporated into a Streamlit application. This process entailed the embedding of the machine learning model into a user-friendly app through which predictions could be made.

Lastly, the output generation and documentation processes were undertaken. Specifically, the output from the application took the form of graphical representation of the prediction outcomes as well as the performance comparison and recommendations.
























