3.1.2. Functional Document

The functional work done during Sprint I focused on implementing the pipeline for data processing and model training.

Firstly, there was data ingestion. Ingestion of the EV charging dataset which had all the information related to charging sessions was done. The attributes contained in this dataset were battery capacity, duration, cost, rate of charging, temperature, type of charger used, and state of charge.

Secondly, there was preprocessing. It comprised timestamp parsing, validating whether the charging stopped after the start of charging, deleting invalid records, taking care of missing values, and managing outliers. The missing values in numeric variables were addressed by replacing them with the median, while missing categorical values were addressed by mode imputation [8].

Feature engineering was the third task to perform. It involved creation of new features based on the existing data for improving model understanding. Some of these features were SOC gain, features indicating efficiency during the charging process, cost-related features, time-related features, and historical aggregated features at user level.

Model development was the fourth task to undertake. Two machine learning algorithms were applied on the processed data; they were Random Forest Regressor and XGBoost Regressor. The output model was then used as the prediction engine for the next phase of optimization and deployment. XGBoost Regressor was chosen as one of the main algorithms for its effectiveness in structured data modeling [6].

